- What exactly changed about your status (employment, residence, financial status, family status)? Ask how that change affects legal claims, remedies, and settlement leverage.
- Are you the petitioner/plaintiff or the respondent in this matter?

What to bring to your meeting (documents & evidence)
- Employment contract, offer letter, severance agreement, non‑compete/NDA, and any written policies referenced.
- All communications with the former employer (emails, texts, messages, LinkedIn/HR messages).
- Performance reviews, disciplinary notices, pay stubs, termination letter, benefits records.
- Any prior settlement offers (written or oral notes), demand letters, or communications about settlement.
- Court filings and pleadings (complaint/petition, answer, motions, scheduling orders).
- Financial records (if damages or support issues are at stake): bank statements, earnings, tax returns.
- Timeline/notes of events (dated chronology you prepared).
- List of witnesses and contact details, plus any documentary or electronic evidence you intend to use.
- Any other court orders or related case files from other jurisdictions (if parallel cases exist).

Core questions to ask Michael Torres about settlement strategy and protections
1. Case assessment & strategy
   - What is our best realistic outcome at trial vs. our likely recovery/exposure in settlement?
   - What are the major legal strengths and weaknesses of our case?
