I can help — this is exactly the sort of prep that makes settlement talks go better. A few quick clarifying questions first so I tailor the list correctly:

- You mention family court in Denver and Fulton County, Georgia. Are there parallel proceedings in both places, or was one a mistake? Which court will handle the upcoming status conference?  
- When you say “my status has changed,” what changed specifically (employment terminated, job change, residence, party status in the case, ability to attend, etc.)?  
- Are you the petitioner or the respondent in this case?  
- Have you already received any settlement offers or proposals (written or oral)?

If you want, answer those and I’ll refine the list. Meanwhile, below is a practical, prioritized set of questions and a checklist you can use at your meeting or call with Michael Torres — plus immediate steps to protect your position.

Top-priority questions to ask your attorney (short list you can read at the meeting)
1. What is our overall settlement strategy and bottom-line goals? (What outcome do we want beyond money — e.g., confidentiality, non-disparagement, rehire, neutral reference, removal of records?)  
2. What is a realistic settlement range based on the facts, law, and similar cases? What is the likely best and worst outcome if we go to hearing?  
3. What are the immediate risks and deadlines I need to know about (statutes of limitation, required filings, dates tied to the status conference)?  
4. How will a proposed release be drafted — what claims will it release and are there standard carve-outs we should insist on (e.g., future unemployment claims, workers’ comp, tax or criminal matters)?  
5. If we accept money, what payment structure is best (lump sum, installment, escrow)? Are there tax consequences I should plan for?  
6. What confidentiality and non-disparagement language is appropriate — and can we limit the effect (e.g., allow disclosure to tax advisors, future employers, or to comply with court orders)?  
7. What obligations will I have after settlement (non-compete, non-solicit, cooperation in future litigation)? How long do these run and how broad are they?  
8. How will attorney fees and costs be handled? If we settle, who pays our fees or are they deducted? If we proceed and prevail, can we recover fees?  
9. What enforcement mechanisms exist if the other side breaches the settlement (escrow, bond, liquidated damages, choice of forum for enforcement)?  
10. Are there any confidentiality or protective orders or preservation obligations I must follow now? (E.g., preserve emails, phones, cloud data — do not delete communications.)  
11. What should I say and not say publicly or to third parties (including social media and to current/former colleagues)?  
12. What should I bring and prepare for the status conference? What will the judge likely ask and what requests might the respondent make?

More detailed legal / settlement-term questions
- Will the release be mutual or unilateral? Will it bar only specific claims or “all claims known and unknown”? Can we negotiate narrower wording?  
- Can we negotiate non-monetary remedies (reinstatement, job reference, removal of disciplinary records, correction of employment file)?  
- If there are restrictive covenants (non-compete), can they be withdrawn, modified, or limited as part of the settlement?  
- Are there confidentiality exceptions for government reporting, future testimony, or court filings?  
- What representations and warranties will the agreement contain (e.g., no pending claims, full understanding)?  
- Does a confidentiality clause prevent me from discussing the case with counsel, family, or future employers? (If so, ask for carved exceptions.)  
- If the settlement includes structured payments, what protections are included if the respondent/employer later becomes insolvent?  
- Can settlement terms be conditioned on court approval? If yes, what’s the process and timeline?

Practical / tactical questions to ask now
- Who will handle settlement negotiations — you, me, or jointly? Who signs off on offers?  
- If mediation is proposed, who pays the mediator and are mediations confidential?  
- Are there discovery steps we should start now to strengthen our position (requests for documents, preservation letters, depositions)?  
- Should we seek a protective order or a litigation hold (to preserve evidence)?  
- Will accepting a settlement affect my benefits, unemployment eligibility, or public benefits?

Documents and materials to bring to your attorney or the status conference
- The employment contract and any written agreements (offer letters, severance agreement, non-compete, confidentiality agreements).  
- All communications relevant to the dispute: emails, texts, direct messages, performance reviews, termination/reprimand letters, HR notes.  
- Payroll records, pay stubs, bonus statements, commissions, benefits documentation.  
- Any settlement proposals already made (emails, letters, notes of conversations).  
- A concise timeline of key events (dates, who said/did what).  
- Names and contact info of possible witnesses and what they can say.  
- Any relevant company policies or employee handbook excerpts.  
- Financial documents if your damages claim is monetary (loss of earnings calculations, job search records).

Immediate steps to protect your position (do these now)
- Preserve everything: stop deleting emails, texts, calendar items, call logs, and back up relevant devices and cloud accounts. (If in doubt, keep it.)  
- Avoid discussing the case on social media, in public, or with coworkers.  
- Save copies of any settlement offers and communications from opposing counsel or the respondent.  
- Draft a clear chronology of events while they’re fresh in your memory.  
- Don’t sign any agreement or make any public statement without first consulting Michael Torres.  
- Ask your attorney whether to send a formal preservation (litigation hold) notice to relevant custodians or the employer.

Preparing specifically for the status conference
- Ask Michael what he will present to the judge and what he needs from you before the conference.  
- Confirm who will attend (you or only counsel), and whether remote attendance is allowed.  
- Request a list of likely orders the judge may issue so you can anticipate deadlines.  
- Prepare a short statement of the current posture (what’s been done, what remains, what you want). Your lawyer can craft this for the court.

Suggested agenda for your meeting with Michael Torres (30–45 minutes)
1. Case posture and jurisdiction clarification (confirm which court(s) control).  
2. Explain “status changed” and how it affects strategy.  
3. Review settlement posture — offers, red lines, priorities.  
4. Go through the top-priority questions above and confirm attorney’s recommendations.  
5. Document and preservation checklist (who does what).  
6. Plan for the status conference (who speaks, what to file, timelines).  
7. Next steps and assignments with deadlines.

Would you like:
- I can draft a short email/agenda to Michael Torres summarizing these questions and requesting a meeting before the status conference; or  
- I can make a one-page checklist you can print and bring to your meeting; or  
- If you confirm which court is actually handling this and what specifically changed in your status, I’ll tailor the questions and checklist to that context.

Important: I’m not a lawyer. This is organizational and strategic prep — use it to guide your conversation with Michael Torres, who can give binding legal advice and specific instructions for your jurisdiction.

