I can help — below is a focused, practical plan you can use to organize your thoughts and prepare for settlement talks and the upcoming status conference. First, two quick clarifying questions so I can tailor this further:
- Which court is handling this matter — family court in Denver (Colorado) or Fulton County (Georgia)? (You mentioned both.)
- What do you mean by “my status has changed”? (e.g., employment status, marital status, jurisdictional status, protective order, custody, immigration, etc.)

Once you confirm, I can adapt the questions and checklist. For now, here’s a general, ready-to-use set of questions, documents, and actions to take with your lawyer (Michael Torres) so you protect your interests.

1) Key questions to ask your attorney (grouped and with sample wording)
- Case status & next steps
  - “What exactly will happen at the status conference and what do you want me to be prepared to say or present?”
  - “Are there deadlines or filings we must meet between now and the status conference?”
- Jurisdiction & venue issues
  - “Is Fulton County or Denver the proper forum for this dispute, and could venue affect settlement leverage?”
  - “Does the change in my status affect jurisdiction or any deadlines?”
- Settlement strategy & valuation
  - “Based on the facts and our documentation, what is a realistic settlement range for monetary and non-monetary terms?”
  - “What are our strongest legal claims and the respondent’s likely defenses?”
  - “What is our BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement) — i.e., if we don’t settle, what outcome can we reasonably expect at trial?”
- Authority & timing
  - “Do we need the respondent’s employer/representative to sign? Who has settlement authority on the other side?”
  - “Is there urgency or a deadline that would change our negotiation approach?”
- Terms to insist on / avoid
  - “What specific terms should we insist on (e.g., payment schedule, confidentiality, non-disparagement, return of documents, reinstatement or non-compete language)?”
  - “Are there deal terms that would be red flags (overly broad releases, gag order that harms professional reputation, onerous non-competes, tax-inefficient structures)?”
- Release language & scope
  - “If we settle, how narrowly should the release be written (claims released, time period, affiliates covered) to preserve any remaining rights?”
  - “Can we carve out claims we don’t intend to release (e.g., workers’ compensation, future wage claims, or certain statutory claims)?”
- Confidentiality, public filings & court approval
  - “Will settlement require court approval or a public filing? If so, what effect will confidentiality clauses have on court procedures?”
- Tax and financial implications
  - “What are the likely tax consequences of the settlement components (salary vs. compensatory vs. punitive vs. emotional damages) and should we structure payments to mitigate taxes?”
  - “If the settlement includes structured payments, what protections (security, escrow, judgment lien) can we request?”
- Enforcement & remedy if they breach
  - “How will the agreement provide remedies if the respondent fails to pay or breaches other terms (attorney fees, liquidated damages, jurisdiction for enforcement)?”
- Non-monetary outcomes & future employment
  - “If preserving future employment opportunities matters, how should that be handled? Is a neutral reference or agreed-upon job reference appropriate?”
- Confidential communications & privilege
  - “How should we handle communications during negotiations to preserve privilege and avoid admissions?”
- Costs, timeline & settlement mechanics
  - “What will the expected costs be if we proceed versus settle? What’s the timeline to finalize a settlement once we agree in principle?”
  - “If I accept a settlement offer, how long until funds are received and the matter is closed?”

2) Documents and information to bring / prepare for your attorney
- The written contract(s) and any amendments, offer letters, severance agreements.
- All correspondence (emails, text messages, Slack messages) with the employer/respondent relevant to the dispute.
- Pay stubs, W-2s, benefits statements, bonus documentation, expense records.
- Performance reviews, warnings, disciplinary records, termination notices.
- Any signed releases, settlement offers or counteroffers already exchanged.
- Timeline of events (dates and short descriptions) — who said/did what and when.
- Names and contact info of potential witnesses and their likely testimony.
- Notes of conversations or meetings (dates, attendees, main points).
- Any internal policies or handbooks that the employer cited or relied upon.
- Court filings and orders (including the scheduling/status conference notice).
- Financial documents if you seek damages (bank statements, lost earnings calculations).

3) What to be ready to discuss at the status conference
- Proposed case schedule (discovery deadlines, motion dates, mediation).
- Whether the parties will pursue mediation or alternative dispute resolution.
- Any outstanding discovery or evidence preservation issues.
- Whether either side anticipates dispositive motions.
- Whether there’s a good-faith settlement posture or a need for a court-ordered mediation.

4) Settlement negotiation priorities & red flags (short)
- Priorities to aim for: clear payment terms (amount, schedule, security), narrow release language, confidentiality tailored to protect privacy without unduly restricting your future, attorney-fee provisions if other side breaches, neutral reference if employment issues relevant, tax-efficient allocation.
- Red flags: overbroad releases, lifetime non-competes, excessively broad gag orders, payment via unsecured installments without remedies, signing away unrelated claims, surrendering rights to future claims you didn’t intend to release.

5) Practical tips to protect yourself during negotiations
- Don’t sign any agreement without your lawyer reviewing the exact language.
- Avoid unilateral communications with the respondent or their counsel without discussing with your attorney.
- Preserve evidence — collect and back up all relevant communications and documents.
- Get any promises in writing; verbal promises are weak leverage.
- Consider asking for escrowed funds or a cashier’s check for large payments to reduce enforcement risk.
- If payment is multi-installment, include interest, late fees, and an acceleration clause if a payment is missed.

6) Logistics & cost questions
- “What are your fees for negotiating a settlement and for finalizing the written agreement?”
- “If we go to mediation, will you attend and is that included in your estimate?”
- Ask for a suggested timeline and what you need to do before the status conference.

7) Next steps I recommend you take now
- Confirm which court/jurisdiction is correct and the status-conference date.
- Prepare the document set and timeline above and send it to Michael with the question list.
- Decide and communicate to Michael your settlement priorities (what you must have, what you can trade).
- Schedule a prep call with Michael well before the status conference.

8) Offer to help
- I can draft a concise email to Michael Torres summarizing the situation and attaching a prioritized question list or a one-page timeline for the status conference — would you like me to draft that now?
- I can also convert the above into a one-page checklist or a script of what to say at the status conference.

Brief legal note: I’m not a lawyer. The above is organizational and preparatory guidance — it’s not legal advice. Follow Michael Torres’ specific legal recommendations for your case.

If you confirm the court location (Denver vs. Fulton County) and explain what you mean by “status has changed,” I’ll customize the questions and draft a ready-to-send email to Michael if you want. Which would you like me to do next?

