I can help — and we’ll make a clear, practical list you can use in your meeting or to email Michael Torres. Before that, one quick clarification so my suggestions fit your case:

- Which court and county is the case actually in? You mentioned a family court in Denver and also Fulton County, Georgia. Those are different jurisdictions and very different procedures. Which is correct?
- When you say “my status has changed,” what changed (e.g., your employment status, a filing, custody/parenting status, domicile, service of process)? The specific change can affect strategy and deadlines.

Below is a prioritized list of questions and items to bring/prepare, organized so you can walk into the status conference and settlement talks ready.

A. Urgent clarification questions to ask your attorney first
- Confirm jurisdiction and venue: Are we in family court in Denver or Fulton County, GA (or both)? If not in the right court, what are the implications and next steps?
- What is the current procedural posture (what filings exist, deadlines, upcoming motions, and what exactly is the “status” the judge set)?
- Are there immediate deadlines I must meet (responses, disclosures, discovery, pre-conference filings)?
- Does the status change you mentioned affect service, jurisdiction, custody, or the court’s authority to approve/enter a settlement?

B. Settlement talks — key strategic questions
- What is our objective for settlement (amount, non-monetary terms, confidentiality, job-related terms)?
- What is our BATNA (best alternative to a negotiated agreement)? If talks fail, what are the realistic next steps and chances at trial?
- What leverage do we have and what does the respondent want most?
- Should we use a mediator? If so, what type (private mediator, court-ordered) and who pays?
- Timing: when should settlement talks happen relative to the status conference?
- Are there any statutory limits on settlement or required court approval?

C. Settlement terms and protections to discuss in detail
- Monetary: exact amount, payment schedule, interest, tax treatment of payments (what is taxable vs. nondischargeable), treatment of attorneys’ fees.
- Releases: scope of release (claims released, exceptions, time periods). Make sure release language is narrow enough to avoid releasing unrelated claims.
- Confidentiality and non-disparagement: scope and penalties for breach.
- Employment-related: references, non-compete/non-solicit clauses, rehire eligibility, benefit continuation (COBRA, pension), final paycheck, unused vacation/paid time.
- Enforcement: how is the settlement enforced (consent judgment, escrow, bond)? What if respondent defaults?
- Filing and court approval: will the court need to approve the settlement? Will the settlement be docketed publicly?
- Future claims and reservation of rights: any claims reserved or excluded from the release?
- Costs and fees: who pays costs, court fees, future fees if enforcement required?
- Confidential side agreements: if needed, consider separate agreements for sensitive items (e.g., confidentiality) but be careful about enforceability.

D. Evidence, documents, and prep to bring to your attorney and to the conference
- Employment contract, offer letters, termination letters, severance offers.
- All relevant communications (emails, texts, messages) with dates and participants.
- Pay stubs, W-2s, payroll records, records showing lost wages/bonus calculations.
- Performance reviews, disciplinary notices, HR complaints, policies (handbook), non-compete or confidentiality agreements.
- Timeline of events (chronological log) with dates and what happened.
- Any prior settlement offers, counteroffers, or mediation notes.
- Witness names and short summaries of what they can attest to.
- Any court filings, orders, or notices you’ve received.

E. Procedural & protective steps to request from your attorney
- Immediate preservation order: instruct relevant parties not to destroy documents or communications.
- Security of communications: confirm which communications are attorney-client privileged and how to share sensitive material safely.
- Ask whether you should avoid public posts/social media about the case and whether to refrain from communicating with the respondent directly.
- Request certificates of service or proof of filings for your records.
- Ask the attorney to outline a timeline of likely next steps and who will handle each item (you vs. attorney).

F. Practical negotiation planning
- Prepare settlement targets: best, realistic, and walk-away numbers (or non-monetary outcomes).
- Identify 3 non-negotiables and 3 areas where you can be flexible.
- Ask attorney whether they want you to make an opening offer or have them handle communications.
- Discuss whether any terms should be confidential or whether it’s acceptable for the settlement to be on the record.

G. Status conference preparation
- What does the judge expect to be prepared to discuss at the status conference?
- Should we file a status report or proposed schedule before the conference?
- Will the conference set discovery deadlines or a trial date?
- Who will speak at the conference — you, your attorney, or both?
- What do we want the court to know and what should we avoid saying on the record?

H. Follow-up and costs
- How much will further negotiation, mediation, or trial likely cost (estimate)?
- What are payment options for legal fees and any likelihood of recovering fees from respondent?
- How will governing law, jurisdiction, or domicile changes affect enforceability of any settlement?

I. Things I can help you with right now
- Drafting a concise list/email to Michael Torres summarizing the changes and these questions (I can draft it for your review).
- Creating a one-page factual timeline you can bring to your attorney or the judge.
- Preparing a checklist of documents to gather.

