I can help — this is a good time to get organized. Before I give a checklist and ready-to-ask questions, two quick clarifying points:

1) You mention “family court in Denver” and also “Fulton County, Georgia.” Which court is actually handling this matter (Denver/Colorado or Fulton County/Georgia)? Jurisdiction and procedure differ a lot — we should be sure which applies.  
2) When you say “my status has changed,” do you mean your employment status (e.g., fired/laid off/reassigned), your residency, or something else? That detail can change what protections or remedies matter most.

Assuming you want a general, practical set of questions and things to prepare for settlement talks and the upcoming status conference, here’s a structured list you can take to your attorney (Michael Torres) or use in your meeting.

Top-priority questions to ask your attorney (use these first)
- Which court exactly has jurisdiction in this case and why (confirm Denver vs Fulton County)? Are there any venue or jurisdictional challenges to consider?  
- What is the judge likely to address at the status conference, and what will the courtroom expect from me or from counsel? Are written status reports or proposed orders required in advance?  
- What is your current assessment of the strengths and weaknesses of my contract claim and the respondent’s defenses? What range of outcomes (including settlement ranges) should I expect?  
- Are there deadlines or actions I must take before the status conference (discovery, preservation, filings)?  
- Do I have authority to accept any settlement offer, or do you need my explicit approval for each counteroffer? What settlement terms would you consider acceptable for my interests?  

Settlement-focused questions (detail to discuss)
- What are my priorities in a settlement (money, reinstatement, neutral reference, confidentiality, non-disparagement, non-compete carve-outs)? Help me rank them.  
- What specific provisions should be included to protect me: scope of release (who/what is released), confidentiality/NDAs, non-disparagement, tax allocation, payment schedule, security for payments (escrow or lump sum), and enforcement/remedy clauses for breach?  
- Should the settlement be filed with the court or handled privately? If court involvement is needed, what does that require?  
- Will we ask for any carve-outs from the release (e.g., for unemployment claims, workers’ comp, future claims for fraud, ethics complaints, or criminal matters)?  
- Are there potential collateral consequences (taxes, unemployment eligibility, public record, professional licensing, immigration/work authorization) and how should the settlement address them?  
- If the respondent offers installments, what protections should I insist on (default triggers, security, attorney’s fees for enforcement)?  
- Do I need confidentiality about the existence or terms of settlement? If so, is the confidentiality likely to be enforceable given the court/jurisdiction?  

Procedural, discovery & evidence questions
- What documents and records should I gather now (employment contract, offer letter, termination letter, performance reviews, emails, text messages, pay stubs, benefits records, HR policies, complaint reports)?  
- Have we sent a preservation notice or taken steps to prevent evidence spoliation by the respondent?  
- What is the status of discovery (requests, responses, depositions)? What’s left and what’s our plan/timeline?  
- Are there key witnesses we should prepare or depose? Who might the respondent call?  

Costs, fees & risk questions
- What are the attorney’s fees and costs so far, and what do you estimate remaining through settlement or trial?  
- If we lose or settle, could I be responsible for the respondent’s attorneys’ fees or costs? Are there fee-shifting statutes or contract provisions that might apply?  
- What is the expected timeline and cost-benefit comparison of settling now versus taking the case further (mediation, trial, appeal)?  

Strategy & negotiation authority
- What is your negotiation strategy and timeline? Will you attempt mediation or informal negotiations first?  
- Do you recommend a mediator, and if so, who and when? Does the court require mediation or is it discretionary?  
- What signals from the respondent should I be prepared to evaluate (lowball offers, structured payments, confidentiality-only deals)?  
- At what point would you recommend rejecting further offers and preparing for trial?  

Practical and personal protections
- What should I avoid saying or doing (e.g., social media posts, contacting the respondent, signing anything) while settlement is discussed?  
- How will settlement terms affect my future employment prospects and references? Can we negotiate a neutral reference clause?  
- If I accept, what will the release mean for any related claims (e.g., discrimination, whistleblower, unemployment benefits)?  

Checklist of documents & prep to bring to your meeting
- Chronology of events (dates, who said what — concise timeline)  
- All employment-related documents: offer, contract, amendments, performance reviews, disciplinary notices, termination letter, separation paperwork  
- Payroll/pay stubs, benefits/401(k communications, PTO records)  
- Emails/text messages relevant to dispute, HR complaints/communications, witness names & contact info  
- Any prior settlement offers, demand letters, or communications with opposing counsel  
- A written list of what you want from settlement (rank priorities) and your bottom line (minimum acceptable outcome)  

Other useful requests to make of your attorney
- Ask Michael to prepare a short memo summarizing: case strengths/weaknesses, likely settlement range, recommended negotiation strategy, and required next steps before the status conference.  
- Ask for a proposed timeline of key deadlines (discovery cutoff, motion dates, mediation, pretrial, conference dates).  
- Ask for a draft settlement term checklist or template you can review (so you’re not making decisions on the fly).  
- Ask how the attorney will communicate offers to you and how quickly you’ll need to respond during negotiations.  

Finally — next actions I can help with
- If you confirm which court and clarify what you mean by “status has changed,” I can tailor these questions more precisely to your jurisdiction and situation.  
- I can draft a concise email or agenda you can send to Michael Torres with these questions, or format them into a one-page checklist you can take to your meeting.  
- I can also help draft the chronology or summarize documents you plan to bring.

Would you like me to (a) draft an email to Michael listing these questions and your priorities, (b) create a one-page checklist you can print for the status conference, or (c) refine the questions once you confirm the correct court and what you meant by your status changing?

