I can help. Below is a focused, practical checklist of questions and items to review with Michael Torres so you’re prepared for settlement talks and the upcoming status conference. I grouped points so you can run through them in a meeting or send them in an email.

First — quick clarifying questions for you to confirm with Michael
- Which court and venue controls this matter? (You mentioned “family court in Denver” and “Fulton County, Georgia” — confirm the correct court and why the case is there.)
- Exactly what changed about your status? Ask him to describe the legal significance of that change and whether it affects claims, defenses, or deadlines.

Questions to ask your attorney (organized by topic)

Procedural / case status
- What will happen at the status conference in two months? (Likely topics: scheduling order, discovery deadlines, mediation, settlement timeline.)
- Are there any filings, disclosures, or reports we must prepare before the conference (status report, proposed scheduling order, witness lists, initial disclosures)?
- What are the controlling deadlines (discovery cutoff, dispositive motion cutoff, pretrial conference, trial date)? Will any of these change if we enter settlement talks?

Jurisdiction / venue / forum issues
- Why is the case in this court (subject-matter jurisdiction and personal jurisdiction)? Could venue be challenged?
- If settlement is reached in one state, will it be enforceable where assets or the other party are located? (Ask about domestication of judgments and cross‑state enforcement.)

Settlement strategy and authority
- What is our settlement objective (best outcome, minimum acceptable, and walk-away point/BATNA)?
- Who on the other side has settlement authority? Have they made prior offers in writing?
- What is the recommended negotiation approach (one-off demand, structured offers, mediation, break-out terms)?
- Should we involve a mediator or neutral at this stage? If so, when and who pays fees?

Economic terms
- What compensation should we seek (damages categories: contract damages, lost wages, consequential damages, interest, attorneys’ fees, costs)?
- How should payment be structured? (Lump sum vs installments; escrow/account; secured promissory note; confidentiality of payment terms.)
- Tax consequences: which portions may be taxable? Should we consult a tax advisor about allocations (e.g., back pay vs emotional distress vs punitive)?

Release and scope language
- Should releases be limited (only current claim) or broad (all claims, known and unknown)? What carve-outs do we need (e.g., future claims, child-support, criminal claims)?
- Request a non-admission clause (“no admission of liability”) and specific language you want preserved.
- Are there confidentiality or non-disparagement provisions? If yes, what exact prohibitions and permitted disclosures (e.g., legal counsel, tax advisors)?
- Are there confidentiality carve-outs for public filings or future legal obligations?

Enforcement and security
- If the respondent breaches the settlement, what remedies do we have and how quickly can we enforce (judgment entry, contempt, attachment, garnishment)?
- Should we require security (escrow, lien on assets, third‑party guarantor)?
- How to handle cross-jurisdiction enforcement if the other party lives or has assets in another state (e.g., Georgia ↔ Colorado)?

Preservation of rights / reservations
- Do we want to preserve any claims or rights (e.g., ability to seek attorney’s fees, enforcement of non-compete, injunctive relief)?
- Should we include a clause saying the settlement is the full and final resolution of specified claims only?

Confidentiality and publicity
- If confidentiality is required, can we still tell limited parties (e.g., spouse, accountant, financial institution) and under what safeguards?
- If confidentiality is breached by the other side, what is the remedy (liquidated damages)?

Costs and attorneys’ fees
- Who pays attorneys’ fees and costs? If we win later, can we recover fees or were they waived by settlement?
- If the settlement includes a fee-shifting clause, ask whether it incentivizes post-settlement disputes.

Evidence, discovery and litigation posture
- What discovery is outstanding and how will settlement talks affect it? Should we pause certain discovery?
- Which documents/witnesses are strongest for our position and which are risky?
- Do we need to preserve evidence, depositions, or send preservation letters?

Practical settlement terms you may want to request or negotiate
- Payment schedule, security, and default provisions.
- Non-admission of liability language.
- Mutual releases with carefully defined scope and carve-outs.
- Confidentiality and non-disparagement with specified exceptions.
- Tax allocation language and who will provide tax reporting forms (e.g., 1099).
- Right to enforce and liquidated damages for breach.
- Stipulation to dismiss with prejudice and retain jurisdiction to enforce the settlement (if possible).
- Allocation of attorneys’ fees/costs and who bears them on breach.

Documents and materials to bring / prepare
- The written contract(s) and any amendments.
- All communications (emails, messages, letters) relevant to the dispute.
- Payment records and invoices, payroll or wage documents (if wage-related).
- Performance records, timelines, work product demonstrating breach or compliance.
- Any prior settlement offers and demand letters.
- Financial statements if you’ll discuss structured payments or security.
- List of likely witnesses and summaries of what they can testify to.

Questions about settlement drafting and review
- Ask Michael to draft a proposed settlement agreement (or review a draft from the respondent). Request tracked changes and plain-language explanations of each clause.
- Ask him to explain any legalese or risk areas in simple terms and provide suggested alternative language.
- Request time to have the settlement reviewed by a tax/financial advisor if needed.

Status conference-specific questions
- Should we be actively negotiating before the status conference or wait until after?
- Will the court require the parties to appear for settlement talk or mediation at/after the status conference?
- Should we file any joint status report indicating settlement progress or intent to mediate?

Checklist to prepare for your meeting with Michael
- Clarify the venue and the “status change” meaning.
- Define your best and minimum settlement outcomes (money, non-monetary terms).
- Gather and bring the documents listed above.
- Decide whether you want confidentiality, non-disparagement, or public admission language.
- Decide how important payment security is and what forms of security you’d accept.
- Ask Michael to prepare a draft settlement agreement and an enforcement plan.

How I can help now
- I can draft a concise email to Michael Torres listing these questions and asking for a meeting or written answers. If you want that, tell me whether to send (or draft only) and any tone preferences (formal, urgent, or casual).
- I can also produce a short one-page checklist you can print and bring to the meeting.

Would you like me to draft an email to Michael including the core questions and request for next steps (draft vs send)? If yes, tell me whether to draft it as a message for you to review or to send it now.

