I can help — this is a good time to get organized. Before I make a detailed checklist of questions, one quick clarification: you mention family court in Denver and also Fulton County, Georgia. Which court is handling the status conference (Denver family court or Fulton County, GA)? Also, when you say “my status has changed,” what changed (employment, residence, marital status, financial status, availability, immigration, etc.)? Those details change what to ask.

Assuming you want a thorough, attorney-focused checklist you can use at your next meeting or call with Michael Torres (Dowd Legal), here are prioritized questions and action items grouped by topic. Use or adapt them depending on the court/jurisdiction and the status-change details you give me.

A. Case & jurisdiction basics (confirm first)
- Which court and county is this matter actually in (Denver family court vs Fulton County, GA)? If there are multiple filings, how do they interact?
- Does this court have authority over the contractual claims? Are any contract issues being handled inside a family-court matter (and if so, why)?
- How will the choice of forum (state/county) affect timing, procedure, and enforceability of any settlement?

B. Settlement process & strategy
- What are my primary goals in settlement (money, re-employment, non-disparagement, confidentiality, clearing records, quick resolution)?
- What are my non-negotiables (red lines) and acceptable tradeoffs?
- What is your recommended BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement)? What’s a reasonable settlement range?
- Who will lead negotiations — you or opposing counsel? Will I be involved in direct talks or only reviewed through you?
- Should we try mediation or neutral evaluation before the status conference? Are there court-ordered ADR deadlines?

C. Financial & payment terms
- How should settlement payments be structured — lump sum vs installments? What are the pros/cons for tax and enforcement?
- Should payments be gross or net of taxes? Who bears tax reporting and withholding responsibility?
- If structured payments: what security should we require (escrow, promissory note, personal guarantee)?
- Will the settlement include attorney-fee reimbursement and litigation costs? If so, how will that be documented and enforced?

D. Release & scope of agreement
- Exactly what claims will be released (state law, federal law, torts, contract, wage claims, future claims)? Make me a list to review.
- Will the release be limited to claims known at signing, or include unknown/“future” claims? (Prefer narrowing if possible.)
- Are there carve-outs needed (e.g., workers’ compensation, unemployment claims, claims the court won’t bar, or claims you want to preserve)?
- How will wrongful-termination or statutory claims be handled (e.g., claims that can’t be waived under state/federal law)?

E. Confidentiality, non-disparagement & publicity
- Should we insist on confidentiality for the terms and non-disparagement clauses? Are there risks to confidentiality (court filings vs private settlement)?
- How tightly worded should a non-disparagement clause be to protect you, while not overly restricting free speech?
- Are there sanctions if the respondent breaches confidentiality/non-disparagement? What enforcement remedies do we build in?

F. Employment-related clauses (if applicable)
- Does the settlement include references, rehire eligibility, or termination language that might affect future employment?
- Are there any non-compete, non-solicit, or confidentiality clauses we need to preserve, modify, or eliminate?
- Will the employer provide neutral reference language in writing?

G. Enforcement, timing, and security
- How quickly must payment be made after signing? Is there interest or penalties for late payment?
- What remedies if the respondent defaults (attorney fees, immediate judgment, lien, contempt if ordered by court)?
- Should we seek the agreement to be entered as a court order or consent judgment for easier enforcement?
- If executed out-of-state, how will enforcement across state lines work?

H. Legal procedure & upcoming status conference
- What do we need to have ready for the status conference in two months (documents, discovery status, proposed schedule, ADR plan)?
- Do you recommend filing any motions or scheduling orders before the conference?
- Is there a benefit to seeking a stay while we negotiate?
- What settlement authority does the respondent have now? Who is the decision-maker on their side?

I. Discovery, evidence & preservation
- What evidence/documents should we preserve and produce now (contracts, emails, performance reviews, paystubs, termination notices)?
- Are there witnesses whose testimony we should secure or interview before settlement talks?
- Any immediate discovery requests or subpoenas we should serve to strengthen our position?
- Do we need a preservation letter or a protective order to prevent spoliation of evidence?

J. Risks, costs & collateral effects
- What are the tax consequences and whether we should consult a tax advisor?
- How will settlement affect other proceedings (family court matters, bankruptcy, immigration, public records)?
- Will a settlement hurt or help any benefits, child-support calculations, or financial disclosures in family court?
- If I accept money, will it be considered marital property or affect support calculations? (Ask if family-court matters overlap.)

K. Drafting & review timeline
- How much time will you need to review and negotiate a draft settlement agreement?
- Can you share or draft a proposed settlement template that protects my key interests?
- How will proposed language be reviewed and approved (turnaround times)?
- Do I need independent counsel for any specific clause (e.g., tax, non-compete)?

L. Fees & billing
- What will your fees look like for settlement negotiations vs trial? Is there a predictable estimate?
- Will you advance costs for enforcement or litigation? How will I be billed if settlement fails and we go to trial?

M. Immediate documents to bring/assemble for meeting
- Original contract(s) and any amendments
- Termination letter(s), separation agreements, and any prior settlement offers
- Emails, texts, Slack, or other communications relevant to the dispute
- Pay stubs, invoices, or billing records that support damages
- Performance reviews or HR records relevant to the dispute
- Pleadings already filed, discovery responses, and court orders
- Any correspondence with opposing counsel
- A short timeline you prepare of key events (dates, actions, witnesses)

N. Tactical questions to ask in the meeting
- If the respondent offers X dollars today, what is your recommendation and why?
- If the respondent insists on a broad release/confidentiality, how can we limit its scope and still get the money?
- What are the strongest facts/evidence for trial, and what weaknesses might the other side exploit?
- If negotiations stall, what’s our next procedural move and timeline?

O. Next steps checklist (practical)
- Confirm jurisdiction and clarify which court is holding the status conference.
- Send your attorney the documents listed above and a short timeline of events.
- Ask attorney to prepare a negotiation plan and a draft settlement template with the protections discussed.
- Schedule a prep call at least 2–3 weeks before the status conference to review posture and offers.
- Decide and communicate your settlement range and non-negotiables to your attorney.

If you want, I can:
- Turn this into a concise one-page “questions to ask Michael Torres” you can use in the meeting.
- Draft an email to Michael listing these questions and requesting a call/prep meeting (tell me what tone and any specifics to include).
- Help you prioritize which documents to collect first based on the “status change” you mentioned—tell me what changed.

Tell me which court is correct and what “status changed” means, and I’ll tailor the list specifically to those facts and draft a ready-to-send email or meeting script.

