I can help — this is a good time to get organized. Before I give suggested questions and a prep checklist, two quick clarifying questions so my suggestions are on-target:

- Which court has the active matter: family court in Denver or Fulton County, Georgia? (You mentioned both.)
- What exactly changed about your status? (e.g., you were moved from employee to contractor, your contact info changed, you accepted or rejected an offer, a subpoena or motion was filed, you switched counsel, changed address, etc.)
- Do you want me to draft an email to Michael Torres requesting a prep meeting or to send documents? (I can draft a message for you to review.)

Below are focused items to ask your attorney, the documents to bring, settlement strategy points, and what to expect at the status conference.

Questions to ask your attorney (prioritize based on your goals)
- Jurisdiction/Procedure & Timeline
  - Which court and jurisdiction controls this case, and why (Denver vs. Fulton County)? Are there any multi-jurisdiction issues we need to resolve?
  - What is the purpose and likely outcome of the upcoming status conference? What should be prepared for it?
  - What deadlines will be set at the status conference (discovery, motions, mediation, trial date)? How binding are they?
  - Has anything in my “status” (e.g., employment classification, ability to work, financial status, custody or family-related status) changed that affects timing, remedies, or jurisdiction?

- Settlement authority & objectives
  - What are my realistic legal claims and likely damages (range)? What are the strengths and weaknesses?
  - What is your recommended settlement target (aspiration), bottom-line (walk-away / reservation point), and BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement)?
  - Should we pursue mediation or private settlement talks first? When and how should mediation be requested?
  - Who will sign a settlement on my behalf? Are there any approvals needed from third parties?

- Settlement terms & protections
  - How should we structure payment terms (lump sum vs installments)? What protections should we require if payment is installment (escrow, promissory note, security, interest, liquidated damages)?
  - How broad should the release be? Should it include all claims past/future, or be limited to specific claims?
  - Should we include a confidentiality clause or allow public disclosure? What are the pros/cons?
  - Do we need a non-disparagement clause or reference language for future employers?
  - Are there non-compete or non-solicit implications to negotiate or remove? Will settlement alter any ongoing restrictive covenants?
  - Should we include carve-outs for future claims (e.g., workers’ compensation, unemployment claims, taxes, whistleblower/EEOC claims, fraud claims)?
  - What remedies and enforcement mechanisms should be included for breach of the settlement (attorneys’ fees, liquidated damages, jurisdiction/venue for enforcement)?

- Financial & tax consequences
  - What are the likely tax consequences of different types of payments (wages vs. compensatory damages vs. punitive vs. emotional distress)? Should settlement allocate amounts to minimize my tax exposure?
  - Will employer withhold taxes or issue 1099/W-2? How will attorneys’ fees be taxed?
  - Should we request net-of-fees payments or specify who pays withholding/taxes?

- Confidentiality, public record, and future employment
  - If confidentiality is required, how do we handle communications with future employers or professional licensing boards?
  - Can the settlement include an agreed factual statement for reference checks?
  - Does the settlement bar me from pursuing future work in the same industry or accepting employment elsewhere?

- Evidence preservation & discovery
  - What evidence do we need to preserve and present to maximize settlement leverage (emails, contracts, performance reviews, paystubs, witness statements)?
  - Are there discovery or document-production obligations coming up? How should I respond to requests/subpoenas?
  - Do we need to send a spoliation-preservation letter or take immediate steps to preserve digital evidence?

- Costs, fees, and net recovery
  - How will attorneys’ fees and costs be paid if we settle? Will fees be deducted before I receive funds, or separately paid?
  - If we proceed to trial and win, what are the prospects for recovering fees and costs?
  - What is the likely timeline and cost to go to trial vs settle now?

- Strategy & negotiation mechanics
  - Who should lead settlement talks — you, me, or a mediator? When should I be present (in person or remote)?
  - What is the recommended opening offer and concessions we should be prepared to make?
  - Are there potential leverage points we haven’t used (e.g., admissions, internal documents, timeline vulnerability, reputation concerns)?
  - Should we insist on a confidentiality carve-out for disclosing the settlement to my tax advisor, attorney, family, or future employer?

- Miscellaneous protections
  - Should the settlement include a mutual release or only a release by the respondent?
  - If the respondent has indemnity or insurer involvement, how does that change terms?
  - If payments might be delayed or defaulted, what remedies (attorneys’ fees, interest, judgment entry) should be automatic?

Document checklist — what to bring to a prep meeting or to upload for the attorney
- Employment contract, offer letters, termination letters, severance agreements
- Any written settlement offers, emails or letters about offers or negotiations
- Paystubs, benefits statements, bonus documentation, stock/unit grant documents
- Relevant emails, text messages, Slack messages with dates and sender/recipient
- Performance reviews, disciplinary records, HR complaints and responses
- Any non-compete, non-solicit, or confidentiality agreements signed
- Witness names and short summaries of how they support your claims
- Timeline of key events (dates, brief description)
- Documents showing damages or financial impact (lost wages, job search records)
- Prior pleadings, motions, discovery responses, and court notices (including the status conference notice)
- Any communications from opposing counsel

Practical negotiation strategy and red flags
- Know your bottom line: decide the minimum acceptable payment/terms before talks start.
- Get payment security for installment deals (escrow, certified funds, lien, bank guarantee).
- Avoid overly broad releases that extinguish unrelated claims or professional rights.
- Watch for restrictive clauses that limit future work or speech (non-competes, gags).
- Don’t accept ambiguous tax allocation — require clear allocation in agreement and consult tax counsel.
- Be wary of “pay now, sign forever” one-sided drafts; insist on mutuality where appropriate.
- Insist on attorneys’ fees and enforcement provisions if the other side defaults.
- Consider confidentiality vs. being able to disclose to future employers or licensing boards — ask for narrow exceptions.

What to expect at the status conference and how to prepare
- Courts typically want to know progress on discovery, settlement efforts, and scheduling needs.
- The judge may set dates for discovery cutoff, expert disclosures, and a trial calendar, or order mediation.
- Prepare a short, factual status update for your attorney (what’s been done, outstanding discovery, settlement offers).
- Ask your attorney whether a joint status report is required and whether they need you to approve or sign it.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft an email to Michael Torres summarizing these concerns and requesting a meeting to prepare for the status conference (I can draft it now for you to review).
- Create a printable one-page checklist for your meeting with Michael.
- Convert the above into a prioritized list if you tell me your top goals (money, confidentiality, ability to work elsewhere, speed).

