I’m not a lawyer, but I can help you organize clear, practical questions and a prep checklist to review with Michael Torres so you’re ready for settlement talks and the upcoming status conference. A few quick clarifying questions before I lay everything out:
- Which court is handling this matter — family court in Denver or Fulton County, Georgia? (You mentioned both.)
- When you say “my status has changed,” do you mean your employment status (e.g., terminated, resigned), your legal status in the case, or something else?

If you want, I can draft an email to Michael with these questions once you confirm those details. Below are suggested questions and preparation items organized so you can use them at your meeting or email.

High-priority questions to ask your attorney (start here)
- Jurisdiction & venue
  - Which court has proper jurisdiction (Denver family court or Fulton County, GA)? If there’s a conflict, how is it resolved?
  - Are there any contractual clauses (arbitration, forum-selection, choice-of-law) that could affect our ability to litigate or settle in this court?
- Status change & immediate implications
  - Given my changed status, how does that affect my legal position, deadlines, and settlement leverage?
  - Are there immediate steps I must take to preserve claims or rights (e.g., preserve evidence, file or respond to motions, tolling agreements)?
- Settlement strategy & authority
  - What are my realistic best-case, likely, and worst-case outcomes (including costs)?
  - What settlement range do you recommend (low, target, high)? What assumptions underlie that range?
  - What are my “walk-away” terms (minimum acceptable) and my priority items (non-monetary terms that matter)?
  - Who will negotiate (you or me), and do I need to give any pre-authorization or settlement cap?
- Terms to protect my interests in any settlement
  - Scope of the release: Will the release be limited to specific claims (e.g., employment/contract claims) or be a broad general release? What should be excluded?
  - Confidentiality/non-disparagement: Should there be mutual confidentiality and nondisparagement clauses? What are typical penalties for breach?
  - Non-compete/non-solicit/ongoing restrictions: Will the employer seek continued restrictive covenants? If so, are they enforceable and reasonable?
  - References/employment verification: Can we secure a neutral/positive reference or specific language about rehire or employment record?
  - Reinstatement vs. severance, benefits continuation, COBRA, PTO payout, stock/RSU treatment: How will these be handled and protected?
  - Tax treatment & structuring: What are the tax consequences of a lump-sum payment vs. structured payments (wages vs. settlement)? Should we get tax advice or a tax indemnity clause?
  - Confidential settlement amount: If confidential, what are the consequences if it’s disclosed? Should payment structure be via check, escrow, or other method?
  - Confidentiality carve-outs: Ensure carve-outs for legal/regulatory reporting, tax returns, or communications with counsel.
  - Enforcement & remedy: How will the settlement be enforced (court order, consent judgment)? Are there liquidated damages for breaches?
- Evidence & admissions
  - Will the settlement include an admission of liability? If so, what are the pros/cons?
  - Do we need to preserve certain documents or testimony in case settlement talks fail?
- Timing & logistics
  - Is mediation required or recommended before trial? When should it occur relative to the status conference?
  - What will the status conference cover and how should I be prepared?
  - What schedule for discovery, motions, and trial does the judge typically set?
- Costs & fees
  - What are anticipated attorney fees and litigation costs if we don’t settle?
  - Will fees be recoverable if we prevail or if settlement terms provide for fee-shifting?
  - Do we have options for fee arrangements (contingency, flat, capped)?
- Confidential/privileged communications
  - How do we preserve privilege over settlement communications and strategy?
  - If I communicate directly with the opposing party or their counsel, what should I avoid saying?
- If settlement talks stall or break down
  - What are our immediate next steps and timelines?
  - Can offers be re-opened, and does any offer carry deadlines or expiration?

Specific red flags to raise on any proposed settlement language
- Overbroad release that waives unknown claims or third-party claims
- Unilateral confidentiality or non-disparagement favoring the employer only
- Broad future restrictions (non-compete or non-solicit without geographic/time limits)
- Indemnity clauses that shift employer liabilities to you
- Tax indemnity that makes you responsible for employer’s tax treatment errors
- No enforcement mechanism or remedy if the employer fails to pay/perform
- Clauses requiring you to pay employer’s legal costs after settlement

Documents and information to bring/prepare for your meeting
- The written employment contract, offer letter, severance agreement (if any), and any NDAs/noncompete
- Termination/resignation letters, performance reviews, emails about employment status
- Communications about the dispute, settlement offers, or disciplinary actions
- Pay stubs, benefits statements, stock/RSU statements, bonus plan documents
- Timeline of events (concise bullet list with dates)
- List of witnesses (names and contact info) and a short summary of what each saw
- Any prior claims or complaints filed (internal HR, EEOC, other)
- A summary of damages you seek (lost wages, benefits, emotional distress if claimed, etc.)
- Any prior settlement proposals or mediation summaries

Practical prep for the status conference
- Ask your lawyer: what the judge will ask, the likely outcome of the conference, and whether you should attend in person or via counsel only
- Prepare a one-page timeline and a short statement of your position to give your lawyer for the conference
- Confirm any deadlines the judge may set during the conference (discovery cutoff, motion dates, mediation)

How to think about settlement options (quick framework)
- Financial: total amount, net after taxes, timing of payments
- Non-financial: references, confidentiality, restrictions, reinstatement possibilities
- Risk: probability of success at trial, time and stress, legal costs
- Enforcement: how easy is it to enforce the terms if breached?

Suggested short script you can use with Michael to start the discussion
“Michael, I want to be fully prepared for settlement talks and the status conference in two months. Can we review jurisdiction, how my changed status affects leverage and deadlines, and the exact settlement terms I should insist on (scope of release, confidentiality, tax treatment, enforceability)? Also, can you review the attached documents and recommend a target settlement range and my walk-away point?”

Next steps I can help with
- Draft an email to Michael Torres listing these questions and attaching your documents
- Create a one-page timeline and damages summary to bring to your attorney
- Draft a checklist of documents to gather

If you confirm which court is actually handling this (Denver family court vs. Fulton County, GA) and what you mean by “status has changed,” I’ll tailor the questions and can draft the exact email to Michael Torres for you.

