I can help. A couple quick clarifying questions before I make the checklist and suggested questions to bring to your attorney:
- You mentioned family court in Denver and also Fulton County, Georgia. Which court/venue actually has the case? (Or are there two related matters in different places?)
- When you say “my status has changed,” can you briefly say what changed (e.g., employment status, custody status, residence, financial situation, new offer from the respondent, etc.)? That will help me prioritize the questions.

Below are suggested questions and a short preparation checklist you can use with Michael Torres to protect your interests in settlement talks and be ready for the status conference.

Top-priority questions to ask your attorney (essential)
- What is the realistic range of outcomes if we don’t settle (including timing, costs, and exposure)?
- Given the facts of my case, what are the strengths and weaknesses of our position?
- If we pursue settlement, what are my key negotiable items and what are my non-negotiables?
- What settlement terms will best protect me (e.g., release language, confidentiality, non-disparagement, non-compete/non-solicit carve-outs)?
- What wording should be included to make the release as narrow as possible (so I don’t unintentionally give up unrelated claims)?
- How should payment be structured for maximum security (lump sum, installments, escrow, certified funds) and what happens if the respondent defaults on payment?
- Are there tax implications from any payment I receive? Should the settlement allocate portions to different categories (e.g., wages vs. damages vs. reimbursement)?
- Will the settlement require court approval? If so, what’s the process and timing?
- If I sign a settlement, what rights am I giving up (including future claims in other jurisdictions or related matters)?
- Can you draft/review proposed settlement language and advise before I sign anything? How much time will you need to review?
- If the other side asks for confidentiality or a gag clause, what limits should we insist on to protect my ability to discuss my professional history where necessary?
- Do we need a specific enforcement mechanism (e.g., liquidated damages for breach, retention of jurisdiction, attorneys’ fees for enforcement)?
- Who will negotiate on my behalf? Do I need to be present for settlement discussions or is it better the attorney handle it?
- If settlement talks break down, what are the next procedural steps (discovery deadlines, motions, trial schedule)?

Additional strategic and procedural questions
- What should we propose at the status conference? (suggested agenda: mediation, discovery schedule, temporary orders)
- Is mediation or another alternative dispute resolution advisable and when should it occur?
- What discovery should we prioritize now to strengthen settlement leverage?
- Are there any pending or likely motions the respondent might file that I should expect?
- How do the recent change(s) in my status affect strategy, settlement value, or urgency?
- Are there confidentiality obligations or protective orders we should seek during discovery?
- Do we need any third-party experts (for damages, forensic accounting, employment practices) to support settlement value?

Practical protections to request in any settlement
- Narrow, explicit release language limited to identified claims and dates
- Clear payment schedule with security (escrow, certified check) and remedies for default
- Non-disparagement clause carefully worded (and carve-outs for truthful statements required by law)
- Confidentiality clause with precise scope (who it applies to, permitted disclosures, court filings)
- Mutual release vs. unilateral release — prefer mutual where appropriate
- Carve-outs for enforcement of the settlement (so enforcement proceedings aren’t barred)
- Allocation of attorneys’ fees and costs for enforcement
- Clause retaining jurisdiction in the court you prefer for enforcement (if possible)

Documents and organization to bring to your meeting
- The signed contract(s) and any amendments
- All relevant emails, messages, performance reviews, termination letters, and offers
- Payment records, payroll documents, and benefits documents
- Any prior settlement offers or communications about settlement
- Chronological timeline of key events (one-page summary + detailed notes)
- List of witnesses and what they can corroborate
- Any court filings, previous orders, or pleadings in the case

Preparing for the status conference (two-month timeline)
- Confirm the conference’s purpose and required filings with your attorney
- Ask your attorney what position to present to the judge (e.g., request for mediation, proposed case schedule)
- Identify any immediate discovery or filings due before the conference
- Prepare a short statement of where settlement talks currently stand and what you want to propose at the conference
- Make sure your attorney files any scheduling proposals or suggested deadlines well before the conference
- Decide whether settlement talks should be intensified before the status conference (e.g., set mediation date)

How I can help now
- I can draft a one-page email or meeting agenda to Michael Torres listing the prioritized questions above. Would you like me to draft that?
- I can format the checklist into a printable checklist you can bring to the meeting.
- If you confirm the correct court and explain what “status changed” means, I’ll tailor the questions further.

Quick reminder: I’m not a lawyer. This is general guidance to help you prepare and communicate with your attorney. Michael Torres is the right person to advise you on legal strategy and specific legal consequences.

Tell me which court is correct and what changed in your status, and I’ll draft a concise email/agenda you can send to Michael.

