I can help — this is a good time to get organized. First, two quick clarifying questions before I create a final checklist you can use with Michael Torres:

1) You mention family court in Denver and also Fulton County, Georgia. Which court is handling this case (Denver family court or Fulton County Superior Court)? Or are there two related matters in different jurisdictions?
2) When you say “my status has changed,” do you mean your employment status (e.g., terminated, resigned), your marital/family status, or something else? That affects what to ask.

Below is a prioritized list of questions and topics to take to your attorney, plus documents to gather and actions to prepare for the status conference and settlement talks.

Priority questions to ask your attorney (start here)
- Jurisdiction & venue
  - Which court has jurisdiction over this dispute and why? If multiple jurisdictions are involved, what are the implications?
  - Will a settlement in one jurisdiction be enforceable in the other?

- Implications of your changed status
  - How does my changed status affect claims, remedies, deadlines, or the court’s view of settlement?
  - Do we need to update filings, orders, or temporary relief because of the status change?

- Settlement goals and bottom line
  - What are my realistic objectives and minimum acceptable terms (must-haves vs nice-to-haves)?
  - What is your recommended settlement range/strategy?

- Scope and form of settlement
  - Will the settlement include a full release of all past, present, and potential future claims? If so, what specifically is covered/excluded?
  - Should any claims be reserved (e.g., future claims, statutory claims)?
  - Lump-sum vs structured payments — pros/cons (tax, enforcement, risk)?

- Confidentiality & publicity
  - Should the settlement include confidentiality and non‑disparagement clauses? How broad should they be?
  - If confidentiality is required, can there be limited disclosures (e.g., to tax preparer, mortgage lender)?

- Financial details & tax consequences
  - How will the settlement be allocated (back pay, compensatory damages, emotional distress, attorney fees) and what are the likely tax consequences for each portion?
  - Who pays attorneys’ fees and costs? Are they paid separately or from the settlement amount?

- Employment-related terms (if applicable)
  - Will settlement affect future employment prospects, references, re‑hire, or eligibility for unemployment benefits?
  - Are there non-compete, non-solicit, or NDA provisions to negotiate or nullify?

- Enforcement & remedies
  - What enforcement mechanisms should we include (liquidated damages, escrow, confession of judgment)?
  - If the other side breaches, what is the remedy and timeline to enforce?

- Timing & deadlines
  - What deadlines do we need to meet before the status conference and afterwards?
  - Is a continuance possible or advisable before settlement talks conclude?

- Process & negotiation logistics
  - Do you recommend mediation, arbitration, or direct negotiation? If mediation, who selects the mediator and when?
  - Who will lead settlement negotiations? Will I be present or represented only by counsel?
  - Are there ethical or strategic concerns about direct communications with the respondent?

- Evidence & case strength
  - What is the factual and legal strength of my case? What are the biggest risks if we proceed to trial?
  - What evidence or witnesses would be key at trial and how soon should we prepare them?

- Discovery & preservation
  - Do we need to serve or request any discovery immediately? Are there records or electronic evidence we must preserve now?
  - Are there potential spoliation issues to be aware of?

- Costs vs benefit
  - What are projected litigation costs to trial vs expected settlement value?
  - Is it worth pursuing further litigation on damages, or is settlement the better economic choice?

Documents and information to bring to your meeting
- All contracts, offer letters, severance agreements, NDAs, non-compete or non-solicit agreements
- Termination/resignation letters, performance reviews, emails/text messages about the dispute
- Pay stubs, bonus statements, stock/option grant documents, benefits docs, severance calculation
- Any correspondence or settlement offers already exchanged
- Court pleadings, orders, case docket, scheduling orders, and the judge’s status-conference notice
- Timeline of events (concise chronology) and list of potential witnesses with a short summary of what each knows
- Any prior settlement demands or demands you plan to make; your proposed settlement terms and “walk-away” number
- Financial statements (if settlement payments or alimony/support interplay)

Preparing for the status conference (two months out)
- Ask your attorney what the judge will expect at the status conference (case schedule, discovery plan, ADR update).
- Prepare a short written case status summary for your lawyer: what’s been done, what remains, and settlement position.
- Confirm whether the court requires a joint status report and who drafts it.
- Agree with counsel whether to ask the court for a timeline extension or an order referring the case to mediation.
- Make sure all preservation steps are documented (so you can show you protected evidence).

Negotiation strategy checklist
- Identify your BATNA (best alternative to negotiated agreement) and reservation point (worst acceptable outcome).
- Decide which items are negotiable (payment terms, confidentiality) and which are non-negotiable (e.g., record of employment change).
- Determine whether you want confidentiality conditional on the respondent performing (e.g., payment first, then confidentiality effective).
- Consider adding staged payments with penalties for missed payments, or an escrow/attorney-holdback.
- Prepare fallback positions and concessions you’re willing to make in exchange for key protections.

Practical questions about counsel & fees
- How will you be billed during settlement (hourly, capped fee, contingency, blended)? Estimate total cost to get through the status conference and mediation.
- If settlement includes an attorney-fee award, how is that treated in our engagement?

Sample quick items to ask during your first meeting with Michael Torres
- “Please explain how my status change affects the case and any immediate filings or protective steps we must take.”
- “What is your recommended settlement range and why? What are the major risks if we don’t settle?”
- “What evidence do we need to solidify our position before mediation?”
- “Do you recommend mediation? If so, when and who would be the mediator?”
- “What deadlines must we meet to preserve claims or evidence?”

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise email or meeting agenda to send to Michael Torres listing the questions above.
- Create a one‑page chronology and checklist you can print and bring to your meeting.
- Draft a short proposed settlement checklist (non-negotiables, financial target, timeline) for you to approve before meeting.

Finally: a quick legal-disclaimer note
I’m not a lawyer. The above is a practical checklist of questions and preparation items to discuss with your attorney. Your lawyer (Michael Torres) will give you tailored legal advice for your situation.

Would you like me to:
A) Draft an email/meeting agenda to Michael Torres with these questions, or
B) Create a printable one‑page checklist and chronology for your meeting?

