I can absolutely help you get organized and prepare the right questions for your attorney. Before I build the most useful, specific list, just two quick clarifying questions:

1) You mention family court in Denver and Fulton County, Georgia — which court is actually handling the contract dispute (or are there parallel matters in both jurisdictions)?  
2) When you say “my status has changed,” can you briefly say what changed (employment reinstated/terminated, moved residence, marital/status change, changed job title, bankruptcy, etc.)? That detail changes which questions are most important.

Below is a ready-to-use checklist of focused questions (grouped by topic) plus documents to bring and actions to take before the status conference/settlement talks. You can use these to talk with Michael Torres or I can draft an email or phone-script for you.

Questions to ask your attorney (short, clear phrasing you can use in the meeting)

A. Case posture, jurisdiction & timeline
- Which court(s) have jurisdiction and which state’s law applies? If there are filings in multiple counties/states, how do they interact?
- What is the procedural timeline between now and the status conference? What filings or disclosures are due before the conference?
- Given the change in my status, does jurisdiction, venue, or applicable law change in any way?

B. Status change — implications
- How does my recent status change affect my legal claims, defenses, damages, or settlement leverage?
- Does the status change create any new obligations or exposure (e.g., tax, benefits, child/family issues, employment-related restrictions)?
- Do we need to file any immediate motions or notices because of the status change?

C. Settlement strategy & goals
- What are our primary objectives for settlement (money, release language, non-monetary terms, confidentiality, employment references, future restrictions)?
- What is a realistic settlement range based on our case facts and comparable outcomes?
- What are the best-case and worst-case outcomes if we go to trial?
- When (if ever) would you advise making a formal settlement offer or demand?

D. Settlement terms & protections
- What standard release language would they demand, and what should we resist (broad releases that bar future claims, admissions of fault, waivers of rights)?
- Should we insist on a confidentiality/non-disparagement clause? What should it include or exclude?
- How do we limit the scope of the release (e.g., exclude future claims based on fraud or obligations not yet discovered)?
- If payment is part of the settlement, what payment schedule or security should we require (lump sum, escrow, promissory note, collateral)?
- How do we handle taxes/reporting for settlement proceeds (gross vs. taxable components)? Should we consult a tax advisor?

E. Enforcement & cross‑jurisdictional issues
- How will the settlement be enforced if the respondent is in another state or country?
- Do we need a judgment (rather than just a contract) to make enforcement easier?
- Will settlement terms be effective across jurisdictions if the dispute touches multiple places?

F. Employment-related terms (specific to former employer disputes)
- Will a settlement include non-compete, non-solicit, confidentiality, or rehire terms? Are those enforceable where you live/work?
- How should we handle references and verification of employment in the agreement?
- If your status change relates to employment (rehired, terminated, changed role), how do those events affect remedies (back pay, reinstatement, lost benefits)?

G. Documentation, evidence & discovery
- What documents and communications are most important to preserve and share with you now?
- Are there steps I should take (or avoid) to preserve privilege and evidence (save emails, preserve devices, avoid direct contact with opposing party)?
- Will we need expert reports or damages calculations? If so, when do we get them started?

H. Confidentiality of settlement discussions
- Can settlement communications be designated “without prejudice” or otherwise protected, and what is your strategy to keep them out of the record?
- Should we use mediation or a neutral to facilitate talks?

I. Costs, fees & practical considerations
- What are your estimated fees and expected costs if we settle versus litigate through the status conference and trial?
- Do we have fee-shifting provisions in the contract that could affect who pays attorney fees?
- What is the recommended negotiation posture (e.g., anchor offer, walk-away number, concessions we can trade)?

J. Authority & decision-making
- What level of settlement authority should I give you? Do you want me to set a firm bottom-line or let you negotiate within a range?
- If a last-minute offer arrives before the status conference, what decision process should we follow?

K. Next steps & conference prep
- What should I bring to the status conference and to our next meeting? (see document list below)
- Will the status conference include settlement discussions, and should we request mediation before that date?
- Are there any pre-conference disclosures, statements, or settlement proposals you recommend we prepare?

Documents and items to gather before meeting / status conference
- The written contract(s) at issue, including any addenda, amendments, or signed offers.  
- All communications with the employer/respondent: emails, texts, letters, Slack messages, performance reviews, termination letters.  
- Payroll records, payment histories, benefits records, invoices, and any documentation of damages/losses.  
- Any prior settlement offers, proposals, or demand letters.  
- Court filings, pleadings, case number, scheduling orders, and any orders the judge issued.  
- Notes about timeline events (dates of breach, termination, communications) and a short chronology.  
- Names and contact details for possible witnesses and any experts.  
- Anything reflecting the “status change” (HR notices, change-of-status documents, relocation/benefits paperwork).

Practical protections during settlement talks
- Avoid direct communications with the opposing party about settlement; funnel through your attorney.  
- Do not sign any release or admit fault without attorney review.  
- Use “without prejudice” or mediation designations where available to keep offers confidential.  
- If payment is promised, insist on secure payment terms (escrow, bank transfer, or payment before release).  
- Limit release scope—avoid sweeping language that waives unknown or unrelated future claims.

Suggested immediate action items
- Clarify which court and governing law apply (ask Michael right away).  
- Provide your lawyer the documents above and a short chronology describing the status change.  
- Ask Michael for a written estimate of settlement range, costs to trial, and a recommended negotiation authority you should give him.  
- Decide whether you want mediation — if yes, ask Michael to propose mediators and timing.

Would you like me to:
- Turn this list into a concise email to Michael Torres? (I can draft and either save a draft or send it if you want.)  
- Or would you like a one-page printable checklist you can bring to your meeting or the status conference?

If you answer my two clarifying questions, I’ll tailor the questions and suggested wording specifically to your jurisdiction and the nature of the status change.

